course: books
lesson: 57
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IX, part 2: Wayfarers All (the swallows and the call of the South)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Back by the river, the Rat finds three swallows whispering on a willow branch; they are not leaving yet, they say, only planning their route, and talking about it is «half the fun».
explain: The Rat begs them to stay just this year; one swallow tells how she once tried staying, and the long sunless nights and the cold with «not an insect in an acre of it» nearly finished her, until she flew south through snowy mountain passes to the hot sun.
explain: As the swallows chatter about the South, something new wakes up in the Rat: a longing to go too. When he opens his eyes, his own river looks grey and chilly, and his loyal heart scolds him for being a traitor.
explain: He asks why they ever come back, and they answer that, in its season, they long for English meadows, the cuckoo and the water-lilies; but today, «our blood dances to other music».
explain: Restless, the Rat climbs a slope and stares at the line of hills to the south, which used to be the edge of his world; now he dreams of seas, sunny coasts and ships beyond them, and lies by the dusty lane thinking of all the travellers who have walked it.
fact: In the osiers (willows with long thin twigs) the Rat sees one swallow, «joined by another, and then by a third», talking «earnestly and low».
fact: The Rat: «What, already ... I call it simply ridiculous.» The first swallow: «we're not off yet ... We're only making plans ... That's half the fun!»
fact: The second swallow: «First, we feel it stirring within us, a sweet unrest; then back come the recollections one by one, like homing pigeons.»
fact: The Rat asks: «Couldn't you stop on for just this year?»
fact: The third swallow tried «stopping on» one year: «O the weary length of the nights! The shivering, sunless days! The air so clammy and chill, and not an insect in an acre of it!»
fact: True science: swallows feed on flying insects, and in a British winter there are almost none, so swallows migrate to warm Africa every autumn and come back in spring.
fact: That swallow flew off one stormy night; «It was snowing hard as I beat through the passes of the great mountains», and then she felt «the hot sun again on my back» and tasted «my first fat insect!» — «never again did I think of disobedience».
fact: Listening, the Rat's «heart burned within him»: «that chord hitherto dormant and unsuspected» (dormant = asleep) began to vibrate, like a string on a harp.
fact: After his daydream, «the river seemed steely and chill, the green fields grey and lightless», and «his loyal heart seemed to cry out on his weaker self for its treachery» (treachery = betraying someone).
fact: Why do swallows come back? «The call of lush meadow-grass, wet orchards, warm, insect-haunted ponds ... the House of the perfect Eaves» (eaves = the edge of a roof, where swallows nest), «the cuckoo's note», and «quiet water-lilies swaying on the surface of an English stream».
fact: «But to-day all that seems pale and thin and very far away. Just now our blood dances to other music.»
fact: Then they chatter of «violet seas, tawny sands, and lizard-haunted walls» (tawny = golden brown).
fact: The Rat looks at «the great ring of Downs» (rounded chalky hills) to the south, «his simple horizon hitherto, his Mountains of the Moon, his limit»; the Mountains of the Moon is an old name for faraway mountains at the edge of the known world.
fact: «To-day, the unseen was everything, the unknown the only real fact of life»: he pictures «sun-bathed coasts, along which the white villas glittered against the olive woods», and harbours full of ships «bound for purple islands of wine and spice».
fact: He lies in the hedge by the dusty lane, thinking of the road «and all the wondrous world that it led to», and of all the wayfarers on it, «out there, beyond — beyond!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «nesh» (North England, Midlands) = feeling the cold easily; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon; «chur» (New Zealand) = thanks, cool; «slay» = do brilliantly; «I'm dead» = that's so funny.
exercise: Where does the Rat find the swallows? | In the osiers on the bank | On the tall roof of Toad Hall | In the wheat with the mice | On the little island in the weir
exercise: How many swallows gather on the bough? | Three | Two | Four | Six
exercise: What are the swallows doing? | Planning their route south | Building a new nest under the eaves | Teaching their chicks to fly over the water | Catching gnats for the Rat's supper
exercise: «That's half the fun!» What is half the fun? | Talking over the journey | Catching the last insects | Teasing the Rat about his poetry | Singing to the field-mice
exercise: What does the Rat find hard to understand? | Wanting to talk about leaving before you must | Why swallows eat insects instead of seeds | How swallows can fly so very fast without tiring | Why the swallows live in the osiers, not the wheat
exercise: How does the second swallow describe the feeling? | A sweet unrest, with memories coming back like homing pigeons | A sharp pain in the wings that only flying can cure | A great hunger that grows worse every single morning | A fear of the robin, who chases them from the hedges and gardens every autumn without fail
exercise: What does the Rat ask the swallows to do? | Stop on for just this year | Take him with them | Bring him back a present | Write him a letter
exercise: What happened when the third swallow tried staying? | Cold, dark, insect-less days broke her courage | She had a lovely winter by the Rat's fire | She was caught and kept in a cage by a child | She went to sleep in the mud at the bottom of the pond till the spring came round again
exercise: Why could a swallow not live through an English winter? | There are almost no flying insects to eat | Swallows hate the sound of the river in winter | The robins chase all swallows away in October | Snow makes their feathers too heavy to fly
exercise: How did that swallow get through the mountains? | She beat through the passes in falling snow | She rode on the back of a big goose | She went round by sea on a ship | She walked through a long railway tunnel under the mountains
exercise: What did she remember best about reaching the South? | The hot sun on her back and her first fat insect | A big party with all the other swallows on a roof | A palace full of gold where she spent the winter | A lake where she learnt to swim at last
exercise: «Never again did I think of disobedience.» Disobeying what? | The call to fly south | Her mother's rules | The Rat's advice | The law
exercise: What happens inside the Rat as he listens? | A sleeping longing wakes and thrills him | He grows bored and wants his tea | He feels angry and jealous of the birds and their freedom | He falls asleep in the warm sun
exercise: How does the river look after his daydream? | Steely and chill, the fields grey | Brighter and lovelier than ever | Frozen solid from bank to bank | Flooded right up to his bedroom windows, brown and fast
exercise: What does his «loyal heart» accuse his «weaker self» of? | Treachery to his home and river | Greed for the field-mice's food | Being lazy about his poetry | Being rude to the swallows
exercise: What does «dormant» mean? | Asleep, not yet active | Very loud | Made of wood | Very cold
exercise: Which of these is NOT a reason the swallows give for coming back? | The motor-cars on the high road | Lush meadow-grass and wet orchards | The cuckoo's note in the spring woods | Water-lilies on an English stream
exercise: What are «eaves»? | The edges of a roof, where swallows nest | The leaves of a willow by the river | The evening hours, just before dark | A kind of fat insect that swallows love to eat in summer
exercise: «Just now our blood dances to other music.» What do they mean? | Right now the South calls them more than England | They have joined a band of singing birds | They have been dancing all morning in the osiers | Their hearts are weak and need a doctor
exercise: What do they chatter about next? | Violet seas, tawny sands, lizard-haunted walls | Snow, ice and the long dark nights at home | The best flats near the Stores for winter | The Wild Wood, the weasels and the stoats, and the snow there
exercise: What hills mark the edge of the Rat's world to the south? | The Downs | The Alps in Switzerland | The Wild Wood | The Mountains of the Moon in Africa
exercise: What does the Rat see in his mind beyond the hills? | Seas, sunny coasts, white villas and ships | Snowy mountains and wolves | A great city with factories | The grim castle where Toad is locked in his dungeon
exercise: «The unseen was everything, the unknown the only real fact of life.» What has changed in the Rat? | Places he never cared about now seem the only real thing | He has started to dislike the sun and the sea | He can no longer see very well with his eyes | He now believes that the river and all his friends are only a dream
exercise: Where does the Rat lie at the end of this part? | In the hedge by the dusty lane | In his bed at home | In his boat on the river | On the little island by the weir, under the wild cherry
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat ask the Mole about the Wide World? | «Don't ever refer to it again, please» | «Let's go there together tomorrow» | «Tell me everything about it» | «It's where Toad was born, in a great house by the sea»
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, who praised «The open road, the dusty highway»? | Toad, showing off his caravan | The Mole, at the end of the trip | Badger, sitting by his warm fire | The old grey horse, in the paddock
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, what «was calling him imperiously» and made the Mole leave his spring-cleaning? | Spring, moving in the air above him | The Rat, shouting from his boat | A letter from Toad | The smell of the river, which he had never seen
exercise: ★★★ Count the things in this part of the swallows' list: «lush meadow-grass, wet orchards, warm, insect-haunted ponds». How many? | Three | One | Two | Four
exercise: ★★★ Careful: «Do you suppose that you are the only living thing that craves ... to hear the cuckoo's note again?» Who is «you»? | The Rat | The cuckoo | The first swallow | The field-mice
test: «Swallows are just nesh, innit, they can't hack a bit of cold.» (nesh = feeling the cold easily) What's the real reason? | In winter there are almost no flying insects to eat | They hate the Rat's river and want to leave him | The robins chase them off every autumn | They get bored with the same river and want a holiday somewhere new
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | One swallow once tried staying and nearly gave up | The swallows leave that very afternoon | The Rat flies south with the swallows | The swallows never come back to England once they have gone
test: Ruby from Adelaide: «that arvo the swallows told Ratty they hate England.» (arvo = afternoon) Put it right. | They love it too, and come home to it in its season | Yes, they say England is drab and they'll never return | They say they hate only the river, not the fields | They say they hate the Rat
test: Why does the river look «steely and chill» after the Rat's daydream? | Next to his dream of the South, his home looks dull | A storm has suddenly blown up | Winter has arrived that very minute | The Rat has caught a fever from sleeping in the damp grass
test: Why does the Rat feel he is a traitor? | Longing to leave feels like betraying his beloved river | He told the swallows' secret to the field-mice | He took Toad's motor-car without asking | He broke a promise to the Mole to take him rowing up to the weir today
test: Aroha from Auckland: «chur, so the Downs are just some hills, nothing special to the Rat.» (chur = thanks, cool) Put it right. | They were always his limit, the edge of his world | They are where his grandfather lives | They are full of weasels he fears | They are the hills he climbs every day before breakfast with the Mole
test: «Ratty slays as a homebody.» (slay = do brilliantly) Is that still true today? | Not quite: today he feels a new longing to go away | Yes: he never thinks of leaving, not for a second | No: he has already packed and gone | Yes: he laughs at the swallows and forgets them the moment they stop talking
test: What would the Rat probably have said about the South at the start of the book? | It doesn't matter; he never wants to go | It is the best place in the world | He goes there every winter | He was born there, in a sunny southern port
test: «I'm dead, a rat wants to fly south!» (I'm dead = that's so funny) What is really happening? | The swallows' talk awakens a real longing in him to travel | He wants to grow wings and fly | He is joking with the swallows | He wants them to carry him there in a little wicker basket
test: Looking back: in chapter V, what called the Mole, and where did it pull him? | His old home, pulling him back to it | The South, pulling him away | The sea, pulling him to a port | Toad, pulling him into a brand-new motor-car for a drive
test: Looking back: in chapter VII, the Rat heard a call in the piping «stronger even than the music is sweet». How is today's feeling alike? | Again a call pulls at him and he cannot resist easily | Again Pan appears on the island | Again he looks for Portly | Again he falls asleep in the bottom of a drifting boat
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Badger said «People come — they stay for a while ... and they go». Who come and go in this chapter? | The birds that fly south, like hotel guests | The weasels of the Wild Wood | The police at Toad Hall | The fish that swim up and down the weir all the year
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Mole liked the winter countryside «undecorated, hard». Who would hate that winter, as this part shows? | The swallows, who need sun and insects | Badger, who sleeps in winter | The Rat, who loves snow | Otter, who loves swimming in the cold winter water
test: Looking back: in chapter II, the Rat told Toad «I am going to stick to my old river». What tempts him now? | The sunny South beyond the Downs | Toad's new motor-car | Badger's underground house | The field-mice's new flats near the Stores
test: ★★★ «his Mountains of the Moon». Why does the author give the low Downs such a grand name? | For the Rat they were as far as the end of the world | Because the Downs are very high and snowy | Because the moon rises over them | Because the Rat once climbed right to the top of them
test: ★★★ In chapter V a smell called the Mole home; here talk calls the Rat away. Which is the right pair? | Mole: pulled homewards; Rat: pulled away from home | Mole: pulled away; Rat: pulled homewards | Both pulled away from home | Both pulled towards home, by the very same smell
exam: Identify the hardships enumerated by the swallow who once essayed to remain throughout the winter. | Long nights, sunless days, chill air and an absence of insects | Excessive heat and a scarcity of water in the river | Persecution by weasels and stoats of the Wild Wood | The loss of her nest to the harvesting machines and the consequent want of shelter
exam: State the effect upon the Water Rat of the swallows' discourse concerning the South. | The awakening of a longing hitherto dormant, so that his river seemed chill and grey | A resolution to remain at home with even greater contentment than before | An immediate departure in the company of the swallows | An indifference to the subject, he being wholly occupied with the composition of his poetry
exam: Ascertain which of the following the swallows did NOT cite as an inducement to return. | The prospect of riding in a motor-car | The cuckoo's note | Water-lilies upon an English stream | Lush meadow-grass, wet orchards and insect-haunted ponds
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Downs? | They had formerly constituted the limit of the Water Rat's horizon, beyond which he cared to know nothing | They were frequently crossed by the Water Rat upon his annual journey southward | They were the residence of the Badger during the summer months | They were wholly invisible from every point upon the banks of the river and its slopes
